   "text": "This structure is octreotide acetate: the cyclic octapeptide D-Phe-Cys-Phe-D-Trp-Lys-Thr-Cys-Thr(ol), joined by a Cys\u2013Cys disulfide bridge, with two acetic acid molecules as the salt.\n\nI read the sequence from the ring:\n- The N-terminal D-Phe carries a free NH2 and a benzyl side chain.\n- The first Cys leads into an L-Phe with a benzyl side chain.\n- Next is a D-Trp with an indole side chain, then a Lys with a 4-aminobutyl side chain.\n- Then comes a Thr with a CH(OH)CH3 side chain, followed by the second Cys that closes the disulfide.\n- The C-terminus is a threoninol, which has a CH2OH instead of a carboxyl group.\n\nFor stereochemistry I used the standard octreotide configuration (D-Phe, L-Cys, L-Phe, D-Trp, L-Lys, L-Thr, L-Cys, 2R,3R-threoninol) rather than deriving every centre from the individual wedges and hashes. I checked the Thr stereo descriptors by hand against the known L-threonine SMILES. The threoninol centres are carried over from L-Thr, since reduction to the alcohol does not change the spatial arrangement.\n\nThe disulfide is written as a ring closure (`%10`) between the two sulfur atoms.\n\nSMILES: N[C@H](Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)N[C@@H](CS%10)C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)N[C@H](Cc1c[nH]c2ccccc12)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCCN)C(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](CS%10)C(=O)N[C@@H]([C@@H](C)O)CO.CC(=O)O.CC(=O)O"
  }
